# Sheela — Portfolio Site

A single-page portfolio for a muralist/graffiti artist. Built as one self-contained HTML file — no build tools, no framework, no dependencies beyond two CDN font links. Open it in a browser and it works.

## Quick start

Open `index.html` in a browser. To edit, open the folder in any code/text editor (VS Code recommended) — you'll be working across two files now: `index.html` for markup and `styles.css` for styling.

No `npm install`, no server required — though if you want live-reload while editing, VS Code's **Live Server** extension works well here.

---

## File structure

```
index.html          — markup + nav filter script
styles.css           — all styling
README.md            — this file
```

`index.html`'s `<head>` links `styles.css` the standard way:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css" />
```

Keep both files in the same folder — the link is a relative path, so moving one without the other (or renaming `styles.css`) will break the page's styling silently. If you ever see unstyled HTML, that's the first thing to check.

Within `index.html`, sections are in DOM order, top to bottom:

```
<nav>                       — fixed top navigation
<header class="hero">       — landing section
<section id="work">         — portfolio grid + filters
<section id="about">        — bio + stats
<section id="press">        — scrolling press ticker
<section class="cta">       — contact call-to-action
<footer id="contact">       — footer links
<script>                    — filter button logic (vanilla JS)
```

---

## Design system

| Token             | Value     | Used for                                       |
| ----------------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `--concrete`      | `#EDEAE2` | Page background                                |
| `--concrete-dark` | `#DFDBD0` | Borders on light sections                      |
| `--ink`           | `#17151A` | Text, dark section backgrounds                 |
| `--chalk`         | `#8B877E` | Secondary/muted text                           |
| `--pink`          | `#FF2E63` | Primary accent (links, highlights, stats)      |
| `--cyan`          | `#00C2D1` | Secondary accent (grid tile #3)                |
| `--yellow`        | `#F5B700` | Reserved tertiary accent, not heavily used yet |

**Type:**

- **Anton** — display headlines (`h1`, `h2`, nav logo). Bold/condensed, does the "poster" work.
- **Inter** — body copy, paragraph text.
- **JetBrains Mono** — captions, dimensions, dates, nav links, buttons. Modeled on gallery wall-text/museum plaques — it's why the portfolio captions (title / location / dimensions) feel intentional instead of like generic card text.

Change any of these by editing the `:root` variables and the two `font-family` values near the top of `styles.css` — everything else in that file references them.

---

## Replacing placeholder content

### Photos

Every image slot is currently a CSS gradient placeholder with a `[ photo ]` label so the layout reads correctly before real images exist. Replace them by either:

1. Adding an `<img>` inside the container, or
2. Setting a `background-image` on the container in CSS

Slots to update:

- `.hero-panel` (right side of the landing section)
- `.tile-art` (each portfolio grid item — currently colored via `nth-child(3n+1/2/3)` rules)
- `.about-portrait` (About section)

Compress images before adding them (TinyPNG or Squoosh) — large mural photos can be huge and will slow the page down.

### Portfolio items

Each grid item is a `.tile` block in the `#work` section:

```html
<div class="tile" data-cat="mural">
  <div class="tile-art"><span class="nozzle"></span>[ photo ]</div>
  <div class="plaque">
    <div class="title">Project Name</div>
    <div class="meta">Location · Year · Role</div>
    <div class="dims">DIMENSIONS</div>
  </div>
</div>
```

`data-cat` controls filtering — must be `mural`, `lettering`, or `live` to match the filter buttons. Add more categories by adding a new `<button data-filter="yourcategory">` and matching `data-cat` values.

### Text content

Bio copy, stats, press outlet names, contact info — all plain text in the HTML, search-and-replace as needed. Instagram link in the footer is currently `href="#"` — swap in your real profile URL.

---

## Known quirks & fixes (worth understanding, not just copying)

**Fixed-nav scroll offset.** Because the nav is `position: fixed`, clicking an anchor link (`#about`, `#press`, etc.) would otherwise scroll the target's top edge flush with the _actual_ top of the viewport — hidden behind the nav. Fixed with:

```css
section[id],
footer[id] {
  scroll-margin-top: 80px;
}
```

This tells the browser to stop scrolling 80px early for any element with an `id`, clearing the nav automatically.

**Scroll clamping on short/late sections.** The Press ticker section sits close to the bottom of the page (only the CTA and footer follow it). Scrolling its top edge to the viewport top would require scrolling _past_ the bottom of the document — which browsers can't do, so they clamp to max scroll and you land further down than intended, in the next section. Fixed two ways: the `scroll-margin-top` above (reduces how far it needs to scroll) and giving the Press section more padding (34px → 64px) so there's more scrollable room around it.

**`mix-blend-mode: difference` on the nav** was tried first for that classic "nav that inverts colors under it" look, but it broke down over the white portfolio grid (unreadable text). Replaced with a simple translucent + blurred background (`backdrop-filter: blur`) that stays legible over both light and dark sections. Worth remembering: blend modes look great in isolation and need testing against _every_ background they'll actually sit on.

---

## Scrollspy (not yet implemented)

Right now nav links just jump to sections — nothing highlights which section you're currently viewing. Real scrollspy would use `IntersectionObserver` to watch each section and toggle an `.active` class on the matching nav link as it enters/exits the viewport. Good next feature to build if you want to practice that API.

---

## Browser support

Uses `backdrop-filter`, `clip-path`, CSS Grid, and `aspect-ratio` — all standard in current Chrome/Firefox/Safari/Edge. No IE11 support (not worth targeting in 2026).

Respects `prefers-reduced-motion` (disables the press ticker's scrolling animation and instant-jumps instead of smooth-scrolling for anchor links).

---

## Before going live

- [ ] Replace all `[ photo ]` placeholders with real images
- [ ] Add real Instagram URL in the footer
- [ ] Confirm email/phone are correct and update `mailto:` / `tel:` links to match
- [ ] Test on an actual phone, not just narrow-browser simulation
- [ ] Run a Lighthouse audit in Chrome DevTools (Performance + Accessibility) once images are in — large image files are the most likely thing to tank the score
- [ ] Pick real hosting (Netlify, Vercel, GitHub Pages, or a site builder like Carrd if you want zero server management)
